    },
    {
      "id": "u000120",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "What the Model Remembers, and Who Owns It",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 8
    },
    {
      "id": "u000121",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "11.5",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "u000122",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "The Long Life After Launch",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "u000123",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "11.6",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "u000124",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "The Edge of the Map",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "u000125",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "A",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "u000126",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "The Math Behind Each Section",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "u000127",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "B",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "u000128",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "Dictionary of Metaphors",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "u000129",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "C",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "u000130",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "td",
      "text": "Where to Go Next",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 4
    },
    {
      "id": "u000131",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "h2",
      "text": "Preface",
      "sourceAnchor": "preface",
      "wordCount": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "u000132",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Picture an engineer who spends an entire week tuning the cache of a busy service. She picks a time-to-live, waits for the dashboard to settle, looks at the hit rate, nudges the number, and waits again. By Friday the hit rate is excellent and she is exhausted. Over coffee, someone asks whether she has ever done any machine learning. She laughs: no, never, that is for people with PhDs.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 69
    },
    {
      "id": "u000133",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "She is wrong, and the rest of this book is, in a sense, an explanation of why.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 17
    },
    {
      "id": "u000134",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "What she does all week is guess, measure how wrong the guess was, adjust, and guess again. That loop is the whole engine. Nearly every model in this book, from a straight line drawn through a scatter plot to the language models that write code alongside you, runs the same four steps. What changes from one section to the next is mostly what is being adjusted, how the wrongness is measured, and how cleverly the adjustment is chosen. The few methods that skip the loop, such as the neighbor search of Chapter 2, are interesting precisely for what they leave out.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 101
    },
    {
      "id": "u000135",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "The difference between her week and a machine learning system is scale and patience. She turns one knob, by intuition, a few dozen times. A model turns anywhere from a handful to billions of knobs at once, often thousands or millions of times, and decides which way to turn each one by arithmetic rather than by feel. It does not try the knobs one at a time, which would take forever with a billion of them: a single backward sweep through the arithmetic tells every knob at once which way is downhill and how steeply, as Chapter 3 explains. That difference in scale is enormous, and it produces behavior that looks like magic. But it is not a difference in kind. If you understand her week, you can understand the machine.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 131
    },
    {
      "id": "u000136",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "h3",
      "text": "Who this book is for",
      "sourceAnchor": "who-this-book-is-for",
      "wordCount": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "u000137",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "This book is written for anyone who is curious to really understand how machine learning works, not just to know its vocabulary. If you enjoy finding out how things work on the inside and have the patience of a good student, you have everything you need. Students who want to enter the field will find it useful, and so will engineers and programmers who already use these systems or are about to build them, researchers from other disciplines moving toward machine learning, and product managers, team leads and investors who need to weigh what a team, a vendor or a startup is promising.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 103
    },
    {
      "id": "u000138",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "You do not need to know anything about machine learning to start. You do not need more mathematics than you met in school: there is almost no formula in the main text. Where a formula would help, it waits in Appendix A, explained in words, and by the time you reach it, it should read like a compact summary of something you already understand. Many examples come from software engineering, because that is where most readers work, but every idea is first introduced through something from ordinary life, a kitchen, an exam, a dark closet, a mountain in fog. If you have never written code, you can follow the book by skipping the occasional programming aside; nothing important hides there alone.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 121
    },
    {
      "id": "u000139",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "h3",
      "text": "How the book is built",
      "sourceAnchor": "how-the-book-is-built",
      "wordCount": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "u000140",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "There are eleven chapters, each a small arc of related sections. Chapter 1 sets up the new way of thinking: learning from examples instead of writing rules, and how to measure that learning honestly. Chapter 2 is the classic toolbox, the methods that still do most of the work on business data. Chapter 3 is deep learning: what a neural network is and how it learns. Chapters 4 and 5 open the hood. Chapter 4 looks at what a model physically is and how data is turned into something it can use; Chapter 5 at how its pieces fit together, what it costs to train and to run, and one road not taken. These two are the most demanding chapters in the book; take them a little slower. Chapter 6 is an interlude about the other learning machine, the brain, and what some leading theories in neuroscience say it does. Chapters 7 and 8 are the modern world: embeddings, attention, language models, image generators and the architectures built from them. Chapter 9 is about the many ways a model is taught, and Chapter 10 about the many ways a well-taught model still fails. Chapter 11 follows models into the world, where they meet experiments, benchmarks, attackers, laws and time, and ends at the edge of the map, with the open questions about where the field is heading.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 227
    },
    {
      "id": "u000141",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Chapters 2, 4, 5, 7, 8 and 10 each end with a deeper section or two, for the questions curious readers ask most often once the main idea is in place: which classic methods the tour of the toolbox walked past, what a model relies on and who decides, how much data, size and computing it needs, how much it can hold in mind at once, why guessing the next word goes so far, which kinds of data do not come as a table, what routines hide inside the training of large models, how sure a model is, and what can be read from inside it. Several of them also own up to what the main sections simplified, wherever an honest answer was too long to give on the spot. They go a little further than the rest of their chapter; on a first, hurried reading you can skim them and come back later.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 154
    },
    {
      "id": "u000142",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Each section builds on the ones before it, and most end with a question that the next one answers. When a question raised in a chapter is taken up again later in the book, the chapter’s last paragraph, a short one called “Going further”, says where; you never need to jump ahead to follow the argument.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 56
    },
    {
      "id": "u000143",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "h3",
      "text": "How to read it",
      "sourceAnchor": "how-to-read-it",
      "wordCount": 4
    },
    {
      "id": "u000144",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "The first time through, read it in order; each section takes ten to twenty minutes. If you have less time, a few shorter paths work well. To understand today’s AI tools quickly: Chapter 1, sections 3.1 and 3.2, then Chapters 7 and 10. If you are a manager or an investor: Chapters 1, 7 and 10; then, for cost and for how models are taught, sections 5.2, 5.4 and 9.2; and from Chapter 11, the sections on reading benchmarks, on privacy, ownership and the law, and on where the field may be heading. If you are an engineer about to build something: all of it, with special attention to Chapters 4, 5, 10 and 11, and to sections 8.4 and 10.5. If you are a researcher arriving from another field: Chapters 3 to 6, Chapter 9, and sections 7.6 and 10.6.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 150
    },
    {
      "id": "u000145",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Appendix B is a dictionary that pairs every picture in the book with the technical term it stands for. Keep it nearby when you start reading documentation and papers; it is the bridge from this book’s language to the field’s. Appendix C suggests where to go after the last page: courses, books, tools to try and a first set of projects, in a sensible order.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 65
    },
    {
      "id": "u000146",
      "chapterId": "chapter-0001",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "One warning before we begin. Every picture in this book lies a little. A model is not an apprentice, a crowd at a country fair or a room full of detectives, and wherever the analogy stops being true, I will tell you. Those are often the most interesting moments, because the place where the picture breaks is usually the place where the machine surprises its builders.",
      "sourceAnchor": null,
      "wordCount": 66
    }
  ]
}
